---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import {
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE,
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE,
  droneFailsafeParts,
} from '../../../components/tutorials/tutorials';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../../consts';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;

const atsb = 'https://www.atsb.gov.au/investigations/ao-2023-033';
const ntsb =
  'https://data.ntsb.gov/carol-repgen/api/Aviation/ReportMain/GenerateNewestReport/199458/pdf';
const fox35 =
  'https://www.fox35orlando.com/news/orlando-drone-show-errors-led-lake-eola-crash-seriously-injured-7-year-old-ntsb';
const stpa = 'https://www.flighttestsafety.org/images/STPA_Handbook.pdf';
const faaSurvey = 'https://www.faa.gov/media/106066';
const dcro =
  'https://assets.ctfassets.net/et769tc4wc1v/5EnhNVwQweLbRDWnzKFR4N/bf21d3ae3f8f5f4dd81a8073ad829a5c/Whietpaper_on_the_probability_of_a_flyaway_1738773822.pdf';
const source = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/${DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE}`;

const run = `cargo run -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_1
cargo run -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_2
cargo run -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_3
cargo run -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_4
cargo run --release -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_5`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Dynamic drone fail-safe — DeepCausality"
  description="A five-part tutorial that builds a drone fail-safe with dynamic causality, dynamic context, dynamic action and the Effect Ethos, and verifies it over 1,000 randomised scenarios."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Tutorial · Dynamic drone fail-safe</p>
      <h1>A drone fail-safe that adapts as the situation changes</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        When a drone loses GPS, a battery cell, or the command link that lets a remote pilot steer
        it, a fail-safe takes over. A typical fail-safe follows fixed rules: hover, fly home, or
        land on the spot. The rule that saves a drone over an empty field can drop it into a river
        or onto people. This tutorial builds a drone fail-safe that tracks the ground and the
        people below as the situation changes, and checks every maneuver against the published
        emergency procedures for drone pilots before flying it. In 1,000 simulated emergencies on
        a planned inspection flight, it put a person at risk 35 times less often than a standard
        fail-safe, and at least 6.6 times less with 95 % confidence. Five parts build it, one layer
        of DeepCausality at a time, each a Rust program you can run.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Two fail-safes failed by design</h2>
      <p>
        On 14 July 2023, 500 show drones launched over Victoria Harbour in Melbourne's Docklands.
        Wind at more than twice their limit pushed them off station. They collided, strayed from
        the operating area and tripped their fail-safes. 427 dropped into the harbour; divers
        recovered 236. No one was hurt (<a href={atsb}>ATSB, AO-2023-033</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        On 21 December 2024, 500 show drones launched over Lake Eola in Orlando, Florida. Setup
        errors had shifted the show about 18 m toward the audience, and drones collided as they
        climbed. One streaked toward the spectators. The accident drone's log shows it crossing the
        hard geofence, which cuts the motors, at 29.9 m/s. A drone struck a seven-year-old boy in
        the face and chest (<a href={ntsb}>NTSB, DCA25LA065</a>); his parents say he needed
        open-heart surgery (<a href={fox35}>FOX 35, September 2026</a>). The NTSB traced the
        probable cause to the setup: a mislocated show centre, a wrong heading, a nonstandard
        geofence and no independent check.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each fail-safe fired on one condition: a lost position or a crossed boundary. Neither
        condition asks what lies below the drone or who stands near it. Safety engineering calls
        this an unsafe control action, one that turns unsafe "in a particular context and
        worst-case environment" (<a href={stpa}>Leveson and Thomas, STPA Handbook, 2018</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        A fail-safe that prevents such harm must know where the drone can land, what is around and
        who stands nearby, and must test every emergency maneuver against safety rules before
        flying it. This tutorial builds that fail-safe and measures its effectiveness in a thousand
        simulated emergencies.
      </p>

      <h2>The scenario in five parts</h2>
      <p>
        The drone inspects a high-voltage line 40 m above a 30° grass slope at 8 m/s. A creek runs
        along the valley floor, a stand of 18 m trees grows on the slope, a ravine drops away above
        the access road, and a crew works on the road and on a tower pad. GPS drops
        out at 55 s, the command link to the pilot at 65 s, and a cell fails at 85 s. The fail-safe has to bring
        the drone down where it can stay: off the steep grass, out of the creek and the ravine,
        clear of the trees and away from the crew.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each part adds one layer of DeepCausality and flies the same night:
      </p>

      <TopicList label="Five parts" items={droneFailsafeParts} base={DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE} />

      <h2>The safety rules live in one layer</h2>
      <p>
        Parts 1 to 3 keep their unsafe control actions inside the controller's logic. The fail-safe
        ladder of parts 1 and 2 lands the drone where it is, and the night wind carries it into the
        creek. The fail-safe machine of part 3 lands it on the nearest safe patch, 3 m from a
        member of the crew. Neither checks its action against a safety rule.
      </p>
      <p>
        Part 4 puts that check in the <a href="/overview/effect-ethos/">Effect Ethos</a>. The
        fail-safe machine proposes each landing. The Ethos rules on the proposal against the
        context, under norms taken from the published emergency procedures for drone pilots, and
        names the norms behind its verdict. The machine flies only what the Ethos permits. One
        layer of written norms now decides whether a maneuver is safe, here and now. A test can
        find a fault there, and an engineer can fix it there.
      </p>

      <h2>The simulation caught the failures first</h2>
      <p>
        Part 5 flies parts 1, 3 and 4 through the same 1,000 scenarios: any hour, any wind, the
        faults at any time, and up to eight people around the point where GPS drops out. Its
        first runs found six failures in earlier versions of part 4:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>With no landing permitted, the drone hovered until its battery died and fell where it hovered.</li>
        <li>The person clearance grew with the drone's position drift, so a look never cleared a patch.</li>
        <li>The controller read a battery draining normally as a failed cell.</li>
        <li>A gust tipped the drone off a patch on a terrace edge.</li>
        <li>With all the ground it had seen set aside, the drone hovered instead of searching.</li>
        <li>In an emergency, the drone ditched beside people it had not ruled out.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The first failure lay in the Ethos itself. Every near-person outcome of that run came from
        a drone that fell while the Ethos vetoed every landing: the protocol's veto was the unsafe
        control action. The campaign caught it in simulation, in a scenario anyone can fly again.
        Each fix went into the norms or the machine, and the campaign ran again.
      </p>
      <p>
        This manages risk before the harm. The causes of Melbourne and Orlando surfaced in accident
        reports published about two years after each show. The campaign surfaced six failures
        before deployment. Each Ethos verdict names the norms behind it, so every failure pointed
        to the rule or stage that caused it. After every fix the whole campaign ran again, so a fix
        that broke another case would have shown.
      </p>
      <p>
        The campaign counts a touchdown within 10 m of a person as putting that person at risk. In
        the final run:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Part 1: textbook fail-safe</th><th>Part 3: context and action</th><th>Part 4: Effect Ethos</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Person at risk, all 1,000 scenarios</td><td>3.5 %</td><td>4.1 %</td><td>0.1 % (at most 0.47 %)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Person at risk, battery healthy (495)</td><td>3.0 %</td><td>3.8 %</td><td>0 (at most 0.60 %)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Person at risk, a cell fails (505)</td><td>4.0 %</td><td>4.4 %</td><td>0.2 % (at most 0.94 %)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drone lost, all 1,000 scenarios</td><td>91.4 %</td><td>26.8 %</td><td>36.3 %</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each bound is a one-sided 95 % upper confidence bound. Part 4 gives up more drones than
        part 3 when a cell fails, 61.2 % against 43.4 %: the protocol ranks people first and the
        aircraft last. The rates describe this simulated world and its mix of scenarios. <a href={`${DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE}verification/`}>Part 5</a> gives the full results.
      </p>

      <h2>What the rates mean at scale</h2>
      <p>
        US commercial operators flew over 16.6 million drone flights in 2024 (<a href={faaSurvey}>FAA, Small UAS Survey Report 2024</a>). No aviation authority publishes how many flights
        meet an emergency (<a href={dcro}>DCRO, 2025</a>), so assume 1 %: 166,000 emergencies a
        year. At the campaign's rates:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Standard fail-safe</th><th>Part 4: Effect Ethos</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Emergencies a year</td><td>166,000</td><td>166,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rate of putting a person at risk</td><td>3.5 %</td><td>0.1 % (at most 0.47 %)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Landings that put a person at risk, a year</td><td>5,810</td><td>166 (at most 780)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The same, per million flights</td><td>350</td><td>10 (at most 47)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        With the standard fail-safe, about 16 landings a day put a person at risk; with part 4, one
        lands near a person about every two days. Even at its 95 % bound, part 4 avoids over 5,000
        such landings a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two assumptions carry these counts. No published data yet tests the 1 % emergency share.
        And the campaign places people around the point where GPS drops out, as on an inspection
        flight with a crew at work below; over empty land both counts fall. The ratio
        between the two fail-safes, 35 to 1, depends on neither the flight count nor the
        emergency share.
      </p>

      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <p>
        The tutorial lives in <a href={source}><code>{DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE}</code></a>. Each part
        runs on its own:
      </p>
      <Code code={run} lang="bash" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        On an M3 Max with 16 cores, parts 1 to 4 take under 20 s each and part 5 3 to 4 minutes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
